AU  - Chen, Ao; Li, Chen; Rahaman, Md Mamunur; Yao, Yudong; Chen, Haoyuan; Yang, Hechen; Zhao, Peng; Hu, Weiming; Liu, Wanli; Zou, Shuojia; Xu, Ning; Grzegorzek, Marcin
TI  - Deep learning methods for noisy sperm image classification from convolutional neural network to visual transformer: a comprehensive comparative study
PY  - 2024
AB  - Background With the gradual increase of infertility in the world nowadays, among which male sperm problems are the main factor of infertility, more and more couples are using Computer Assisted Sperm Analysis to assist in the analysis and treatment of infertility. Meanwhile, the rapid development of deep learning has led to strong results in image classification tasks. However, the classification of sperm images has not been well studied in current deep learning methods, and the sperm images are often disturbed by noise in practical Computer Assisted Sperm Analysis applications. The purpose of this paper is to come to investigate the anti-noise robustness of deep learning classification methods on sperm images. Methods The SVIA dataset is a publicly large-scale sperm dataset containing three subsets. In this paper, we used subset-C, which provides more than 125,000 independent images of sperms and impurities, including 121,401 sperm images and 4,479 impurity images. In order to investigate the anti-noise robustness of deep learning classification methods on sperm images. we conducted a comprehensive comparative study of sperm images using many Convolutional Neural Network and Visual Transformer deep learning methods to find the deep learning model with the most stable anti-noise robustness. Results This study proved that ViT had strong robustness for the classification of the tiny object (sperm and impurity) image datasets under some conventional noises and adversarial attacks. In particular, under the influence of Poisson noise, the accuracy changed from 91.45% to 91.08%, the precision of impurity changes from 92.7% to 91.3%, the recall of impurity changed from 88.8% to 89.5%, and the F1-score of impurity changed 90.7% to 90.4%. Meanwhile, the precision of sperm changed from 90.9% to 90.5%, the recall of sperm changed from 92.5% to 93.8%, and the F1-score of sperm changed from 92.1% to 90.4%. Conclusion We obtain two conclusions: the image classification effects of sperm images are strongly affected by noise in current deep learning methods; the robustness against noise of VT methods based on global information is stronger than that of CNN methods based on local information, indicating that the robustness against noise is mainly reflected in global information.
