AU  - Nguyen, Viet Dung; Nguyen, Duc Hieu; Duong, Le Minh
TI  - Omnidirectional Camera-Based Fall Detection Using Deep Learning
PY  - 2025
AB  - Falls among the elderly pose a major health risk, especially for individuals living alone. Automated fall detection systems are crucial for timely intervention. Omnidirectional cameras offer wide coverage but introduce geometric distortion, posing challenges for conventional vision models. This study evaluates fall detection performance using the BOMNI dataset, focusing on Scenario \#1, which includes various human actions. We compare different preprocessing pipelines and deep learning models, including DETR-ResNet50 and YOLOv11. Our bestperforming model, YOLOv11-M trained on preprocessed data with undistortion and background subtraction, achieved a precision of 0.978, recall of 0.957, F1-score of 0.967, and mAP@ 50 of 0.982 surpassing existing methods on the same dataset. DETRResNet50 showed strong performance in detecting the “fainted” class with high AP, while YOLOv11 demonstrated superior overall robustness. These results confirm the effectiveness of deep learning, particularly YOLO-based models, for real-time fall detection in omnidirectional video. Future work will focus on incorporating temporal dynamics and optimizing models for real-world deployment.
