AU  - Akhtar, Sania; Hanif, Muhammad; Rashid, Ahmar; Khalil, Akhtar; Ahmad Khan, Ejaz; Melih Saraoglu, Hamdi
TI  - YOLOv8–YOLOv11 Ensemble With Box Fusion for Enhanced Detection of Underrepresented Urine Sediment Particles
PY  - 2025
AB  - Urine sediment microscopy plays a vital role in diagnosing urinary tract and kidney-related disorders. Although deep learning-based detectors such as the YOLO family have shown promising results in the effective detection of urine sediments, the accurate detection of underrepresented or morphologically complex particles remains a challenging task. To address these limitations, we proposed an ensemble-based object detection framework that integrates YOLOv8 subtle patterns learning with the YOLOv11 performance in imbalanced data. Our approach employs both Weighted Box Fusion (WBF) and Non-Maximum Suppression (NMS) to combine predictions from both models. The WBF fuse high-confidence predictions from both detectors, improving sensitivity for rare classes, while the NMS filters ensure compact results. Evaluated on a urinary sediment dataset comprising 7,201 expert-annotated images across 14 clinically relevant classes, the WBF-based ensemble achieves 92% AP@0.5 and an average precision of 86% with an inference time of 57.1 ms per image, while the NMS-based ensemble attains 91% AP@0.5 and an average precision of 84.9% with an inference time of 59.1 ms per image. Both fusion methods outperform the standalone YOLOv11m and YOLOv8m detectors, and WBF shows notable improvements in minority-class sensitivity for categories such as WBC clumps, sperm, and budding yeast. These results highlight the effectiveness of ensemble fusion strategies in delivering accurate and reliable urinalysis in clinically challenging, data-scarce environments.
