AU  - Alshdaifat, Eman Hussein; Amin, Mohammad; Abu Mhanna, Hamad Yahia; Malkawi, Majd; Al-Mnayyis, Asma'a Mohammad; Gharaibeh, Hasan; Akhdar, Hanan Fawaz; Madain, Rola; Alhatamleh, Salem; Al-Qablan, Tamara Amjad; Alsalmanc, Neda'a; Alomar, Alaa; Mohammad, Qutaiba; Sindiani, Amer Mahmoud
TI  - RETRACTED: (KAUH-BCUSD): Computer-aided breast cancer diagnosis using transformer and CNN using ultrasound dataset
PY  - 2025
AB  - Breast cancer is the leading cause of death among women worldwide. Early detection of breast cancer helps reduce global mortality rates. The current era is characterized by the rapid spread of computer-aided diagnosis (CAD) technologies in the medical field. This research analyzes ultrasound-derived medical images of breast cancer. The researchers suggest that breast cancer risk factors are associated with cultural and societal influences. Therefore, there is a need for a local dataset that accurately reflects breast cancer in our region to facilitate the development and evaluation of automated CAD systems for breast cancer. This study presents a novel dataset for breast cancer ultrasound diagnosis from King Abdullah University Hospital (KAUH). The KAUH-BCUSD dataset is the first dataset in the Hashemite Kingdom of Jordan to include multiple breast cancer ultrasound examinations. It consists of 6,159 images collected from 5,000 cases. Physicians from KAUH annotated, evaluated, and classified the dataset into benign and malignant images. Breast ultrasound images achieve exceptional results in breast cancer classification and diagnosis when combined with machine learning. The CNN_ViT model, which combines a convolutional neural network (CNN) and a vision transformer (ViT), was developed and evaluated on the KAUH-BCUSD dataset. Eight pre-trained neural network models, namely Vgg16, Vgg19, MobileNetV2, ResNet50, InceptionV3, Xception, DenseNet121, and ConvNeXt, were also evaluated on the KAUH-BCUSD dataset. The proposed CNN_ViT model outperformed the pre-trained models, achieving an accuracy of 0.9512. It also performed better in recall and F1 score, reaching 0.9754 and 0.9524, respectively. In conclusion, these results demonstrate the potential of the proposed model in improving breast cancer classification, indicating its reliability in real-world clinical applications. Future work will focus on further improving the model's performance and exploring its integration with other diagnostic tools to enhance early detection and treatment planning. • A novel breast cancer ultrasound dataset (KAUH-BCUSD) was developed from King Abdullah University Hospital, comprising 6,159 images from 5,000 cases. • The dataset, annotated and classified by expert physicians, includes benign and malignant breast ultrasound images to support the development of local computer-aided diagnosis (CAD) systems. • A hybrid CNN_ViT model, integrating Convolutional Neural Networks (CNN) and Vision Transformers (ViT), was proposed to enhance breast cancer classification. • The CNN_ViT model was benchmarked against eight well-known pre-trained models (Vgg16, Vgg19, MobileNetV2, ResNet50, InceptionV3, Xception, DenseNet121, and ConvNeXt). • The proposed CNN_ViT model outperformed all comparative models, achieving an accuracy of 95.12%, recall of 97.54%, and F1-score of 95.24% on the KAUH-BCUSD dataset. • The results demonstrate the potential of the CNN_ViT model for reliable clinical breast cancer diagnosis, with future work focusing on further performance improvements and integration with other diagnostic tools.
