AU  - Littidej, Patiwat; Pumhirunroj, Benjamabhorn; Slack, Donald
TI  - An Alternative Model for Predicting Rubber Yield in High-Density Plots in Ecological Areas Adjacent to the Mekong River Using Forest-Based Classification and Regression on a Hexagonal Grid
PY  - 2025
AB  - This study addresses the challenge of predicting rubber yield in high-density plots near the Mekong River, where traditional rectangular-grid methods often introduce spatial bias and misrepresent ecological gradients. To overcome these limitations, we propose an alternative spatial modeling approach using Forest-Based Classification and Regression (FCR) applied to hexagonal grids, which better align with average farm plot sizes and enhance spatial continuity. The methodology involves converting raster data into hexagonal grids and integrating eight spatial and remote sensing variables. Among these, six variables precipitation (X8), aspect (X5), normalized difference vegetation index (NDVI) (X7), normalized difference water index (NDWI) (X6), topographic wetness index (TWI) (X4), and proximity to the main river (X2) were identified as significant in the FCR model. Notably, only precipitation and TWI exhibited strong linear correlations with rubber yield and were included in the best-performing model. The resulting model (Model-1) achieved a coefficient of determination (R²) of 0.815 and a root mean square error (RMSE) of 58.42 kg/ha, demonstrating its effectiveness in capturing non-linear relationships while maintaining interpretability through key hydrological indicators. Beyond statistical performance, the model provides practical value for agricultural planning. By identifying spatial zones with favorable hydrological conditions, it facilitates targeted interventions such as optimized irrigation, plot selection, and resource allocation. This can assist smallholder farmers and policymakers in improving yield outcomes, prioritizing high-potential areas, and mitigating risks associated with climate variability. Rather than proposing a universal framework for all crops, this study specifically focuses on rubber yield prediction within a distinct ecological zone. While the hexagonal-grid approach may offer conceptual advantages for other crops, its broader applicability necessitates further validation. These findings underscore the importance of spatial restructuring and targeted variable selection in enhancing machine learning-based yield prediction.
