AU  - Peng, Yanfei; Guo, Hongyou; Chen, Zuyi
TI  - TransEdgeNet: a multi-branch edge-aware CNN-transformer network for medical image segmentation
PY  - 2026
AB  - <jats:title>Abstract</jats:title>
                  <jats:p>Medical image segmentation is a core task in computer-aided diagnosis, with the objective of accurately delineating anatomical structures from imaging data. In recent years, Transformers have garnered significant attention due to their powerful global context modeling capabilities, the self-attention mechanism can effectively model long-range dependencies within images, thereby partially overcoming the limitations of Convolutional Neural Networks (CNN) in capturing global features. Inspired by this, hybrid architectures that combine CNN with Transformers have demonstrated promising application potential in medical image segmentation. However, most existing methods typically process high-level semantic information and edge structural information uniformly, neglecting the complementary roles of different feature modalities in segmentation tasks. This, to a certain extent, restricts the improvement of model performance. To address the aforementioned issues, this paper proposes a novel parallel architecture called TransEdgeNet, which integrates CNN and Swin Transformers to fully leverage the complementarity of multimodal features and enhance edge information representation. Specifically, TransEdgeNet employs a multi-branch encoder design, comprising three functionally complementary branches: (1) The CNN branch is built upon the Multi-Scale Extraction Residual Block (MSERes Block) designed in this paper, which is employed to capture local detailed features of the image. (2) The Swin branch models long-range dependencies through the shifted-window self-attention mechanism of the Swin Transformer, thereby capturing global contextual features. (3) The Edge branch focuses on perceiving and enhancing edge-structure features. By introducing a gating mechanism, it adaptively modulates the response strength of high-level semantic feature maps at different spatial locations, thereby improving the representational capacity in boundary regions and enabling effective routing and supplementation of semantic information. During the decoding phase, the CBAM attention mechanism is introduced to facilitate efficient fusion of features from the Swin and CNN branches. Additionally, cross-branch skip connections are employed to enhance information interaction between features at different levels, thereby improving the decoder’s ability to integrate multiscale features and edge structures. Experiments were conducted on three medical image segmentation datasets: Synapse, ACDC, and AVT datasets. The results demonstrate that the proposed multi-branch encoding architecture exhibits significant advantages in mitigating multimodal feature interference. Compared with existing mainstream segmentation methods, TransEdgeNet achieves superior segmentation accuracy, particularly in multi-organ segmentation tasks, where it substantially improves the Dice similarity coefficient and effectively reduces the Hausdorff distance (HD), validating its advanced and practical value in medical image segmentation tasks.</jats:p>
